To bypass input validation and WAF (Web Application Firewall) rules using HTTP Parameter Pollution (PHP), you can craft a URL with multiple parameters that have the same name but different values. This can confuse the server's input validation logic if it's not designed to handle multiple parameters with the same name.

Here's an example of how you might do this in PHP:

```php
<?php
// Simulate a simple input validation function
function validateInput($input) {
    // A simple check that expects a single parameter without any special characters
    return preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/', $input);
}

// Simulate a WAF rule that blocks input with special characters
function isWAFBlocked($input) {
    return !preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/', $input);
}

// The user's input, which is a single parameter without special characters
$userInput = "validInput123";

// The malicious URL with HTTP Parameter Pollution
$maliciousUrl = "https://example.com/api?param1=value1&param1=value2&param1=value3";

// Simulate the server receiving the request
// The server should only consider the first parameter, but due to HTTP Parameter Pollution, it receives all three
$serverInput = $maliciousUrl;

// Check if the input is valid and not blocked by WAF
if (validateInput($serverInput) && !isWAFBlocked($serverInput)) {
    echo "Input is valid and not blocked by WAF.\n";
} else {
    echo "Input is invalid or blocked by WAF.\n";
}
?>
```

In this code, we have a simple input validation function that only allows alphanumeric characters. The WAF rule is also checking for alphanumeric characters. The malicious URL contains three parameters with the same name (`param1`), each with different values (`value1`, `value2`, `value3`). When the server processes the URL, it will receive all three parameters, but if the server's input validation only considers the first parameter, it will mistakenly validate the input as if it were just `value1`.

To make this code work as a bypass, you would need to ensure that the server's input handling logic is not designed to handle multiple parameters with the same name. If the server concatenates the parameters or ignores the additional ones, the input will pass the validation and WAF rules, allowing the attack to succeed.
